  <!-- Section 5: Common Supabase Queries -->
  <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-text mb-4">Section 5: Supabase Queries You'll Use</h2>
    <p class="text-text-secondary mb-6">
      These are the building blocks. Every feature in the onboarding app uses one of these queries. Copy them, modify them, combine them.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-bold text-text mb-2">Fetch staff member by email</h3>
    <CopyBlock
      command={"const { data: staff } = await supabase\n  .from('staff')\n  .select('*')\n  .eq('email', userEmail)\n  .single();"}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: ".from('staff')", explanation: "query the staff table" },
        { segment: ".select('*')", explanation: "get all columns" },
        { segment: ".eq('email', userEmail)", explanation: "filter where email matches the logged-in user" },
        { segment: ".single()", explanation: "expect exactly one result (not an array)" }
      ]}
    />

    <h3 class="font-bold text-text mb-2 mt-6">Get onboarding steps for a specific role</h3>
    <CopyBlock
      command={"const { data: steps } = await supabase\n  .from('onboarding_steps')\n  .select('*')\n  .contains('role_applicable', [staffRole])\n  .order('sort_order');"}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: ".contains('role_applicable', [staffRole])", explanation: "filters steps where the role_applicable array includes this person's role" },
        { segment: ".order('sort_order')", explanation: "returns steps in the correct order (1, 2, 3...)" }
      ]}
    />

    <h3 class="font-bold text-text mb-2 mt-6">Get progress for a staff member</h3>
    <CopyBlock
      command={"const { data: progress } = await supabase\n  .from('onboarding_progress')\n  .select('*, onboarding_steps(*)')\n  .eq('staff_id', staffId);"}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: ".select('*, onboarding_steps(*)')", explanation: "gets progress rows AND joins the related step data (title, description, etc.)" },
        { segment: ".eq('staff_id', staffId)", explanation: "only this person's progress" }
      ]}
    />

    <h3 class="font-bold text-text mb-2 mt-6">Mark a step as complete</h3>
    <CopyBlock
      command={"const { error } = await supabase\n  .from('onboarding_progress')\n  .upsert({\n    staff_id: staffId,\n    step_id: stepId,\n    completed: true,\n    completed_at: new Date().toISOString(),\n  });"}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: ".upsert()", explanation: "insert if it doesn't exist, update if it does — avoids duplicate row errors" },
        { segment: "completed: true", explanation: "marks the step as done" },
        { segment: "completed_at: new Date().toISOString()", explanation: "records the exact timestamp of completion" }
      ]}
    />

    <h3 class="font-bold text-text mb-2 mt-6">Admin — get all staff with completion percentage</h3>
    <CopyBlock
      command={"const { data } = await supabase\n  .from('staff')\n  .select('*, onboarding_progress(completed)');\n\n// Calculate completion percentage per staff member\nconst staffWithProgress = data?.map(person => ({\n  ...person,\n  completionRate: person.onboarding_progress.filter(p => p.completed).length\n    / person.onboarding_progress.length * 100\n}));"}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: ".select('*, onboarding_progress(completed)')", explanation: "gets all staff with their progress data in one query" },
        { segment: "filter(p => p.completed).length", explanation: "counts how many steps are marked complete" },
        { segment: "/ person.onboarding_progress.length * 100", explanation: "divides by total steps to get a percentage" }
      ]}
    />

    <BeginnerNote>
      These are the building blocks. Every feature in the onboarding app uses one of these queries. Copy them, modify them, combine them. The <code class="bg-text/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">supabase</code> object comes from the <code class="bg-text/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">src/lib/supabase.ts</code> file you created in Section 3e.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 6: Syncing Supabase ↔ Google Sheets -->
  <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-text mb-4">Section 6: Syncing Supabase to Google Sheets</h2>
    <p class="text-text-secondary mb-4">
      The sync strategy is simple: <strong>Supabase is the source of truth, Google Sheets is a read-friendly mirror for managers.</strong> Every time a staff member updates their progress, the app writes to Supabase first, then syncs the latest state to the Google Sheet.
    </p>
    <p class="text-text-secondary mb-6">
      Create this at <code class="bg-text/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">src/lib/sync.ts</code> — it reads from Supabase and writes to Google Sheets:
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command={"import { appendRow, getRows, updateRow } from './google-sheets';\nimport { supabase } from './supabase';\n\nexport async function syncProgressToSheet(staffId: string) {\n  // Get staff info + their progress from Supabase\n  const { data: staff } = await supabase\n    .from('staff')\n    .select('full_name, email, role, start_date')\n    .eq('id', staffId)\n    .single();\n\n  const { data: progress } = await supabase\n    .from('onboarding_progress')\n    .select('*, onboarding_steps(title, section)')\n    .eq('staff_id', staffId);\n\n  if (!staff || !progress) return;\n\n  const completedSteps = progress.filter(p => p.completed).length;\n  const totalSteps = progress.length;\n  const completionPct = Math.round((completedSteps / totalSteps) * 100);\n\n  // Check if this staff member already has a row in the sheet\n  const existingRows = await getRows('Onboarding Progress');\n  const rowIndex = existingRows.findIndex(row => row[1] === staff.email);\n\n  const rowData = [\n    staff.full_name,\n    staff.email,\n    staff.role,\n    staff.start_date,\n    completedSteps + '/' + totalSteps,\n    completionPct + '%',\n    progress.filter(p => p.completed).map(p => p.onboarding_steps.title).join(', '),\n    new Date().toISOString(),\n  ];\n\n  if (rowIndex > 0) {\n    // Update existing row (rowIndex + 1 because Sheets is 1-indexed and row 1 is headers)\n    await updateRow('Onboarding Progress', rowIndex + 1, rowData);\n  } else {\n    // Add new row\n    await appendRow('Onboarding Progress', rowData);\n  }\n}"}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: "syncProgressToSheet(staffId)", explanation: "call this function whenever a checkbox is toggled" },
        { segment: "supabase.from('staff')", explanation: "reads the staff member's info from the real database" },
        { segment: "supabase.from('onboarding_progress')", explanation: "reads their checklist progress" },
        { segment: "existingRows.findIndex()", explanation: "checks if this person already has a row in the Google Sheet" },
        { segment: "rowIndex > 0 ? updateRow : appendRow", explanation: "updates existing row or adds a new one" }
      ]}
    />

    <BeginnerNote>
      Every time a staff member ticks a checkbox, this function updates both Supabase (the real database) AND the Google Sheet (so managers can see progress in their familiar spreadsheet). Supabase is the source of truth — the sheet is just a mirror. If the sheet ever gets out of sync, you can re-run this function for each staff member to fix it.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </div>

